In real life, scientists often do not worry about the absolute enthalpy of a system.  We never say "the enthalpy is now 10,000 Joules".  

All that really matters is how much it *changes* during a chemical or physical process.  We say that the enthalpy during a reaction *changed* by 10,000 Joules.  We don't really care what the number is at the start, and what the number is at the end.  We only care about how much the number changed.

That's why you'll almost never hear about absolute enthalpy.

Strangely, entropy is different.  It can be established on an absolute scale, because we say that the entropy of a perfectly ordered solid at absolute zero (about -273C) is zero.

